comparisons are (rows, sum(id), distinct notes) plus PRAGMA integrity_check.
A count alone cannot see a restore that returns the right number of wrong
rows, and a well-formed database can still be the wrong one.

  source:                      70|2485|70   (30 rows, then 40 more)  integrity: ok
  content captured at the mid-history position, before the next batch existed:
                               30|465|30   integrity: ok
  segments in the replica:      3
  1. latest restore, exit 0:     70|2485|70   integrity: ok
  2. mid-history -txid 0000000000000002, exit 0: 30|465|30   integrity: ok
     point-in-time -timestamp 2026-10-14T10:12:08Z, exit 0: 30|465|30   integrity: ok
     both must equal the content captured at that position. Comparing them
     with EACH OTHER is not the same check: two selectors can agree on the
     same wrong earlier point, and only the captured digest rules that out.
     selectors-agree: 1 (1 = the two restores agree).
     the point is the SECOND segment, not the first. The first row of
     `litestream ltx` is the base snapshot, whose max_txid is the very start
     of history: -txid against it restores an EMPTY database (0 rows) with
     exit 0. That is a correct restore of the wrong point, and reading it as
     a broken drill is what a first pass here did.
     the boundary: -timestamp equal to a segment's own created time answers
     "no matching backup files available". Measured, and it is not an empty
     replica -- the same replica restores fully one second later.

  3. corrupt-artifact cases, over the bucket's own objects
     (4 downloaded through the signed API; the service's copy untouched):
     one byte flipped deep inside the newest level-0 artifact: rejected|1|missing
     an artifact truncated to 10%:                            rejected|1|missing
     every artifact reduced to two bytes:                     rejected|1|missing
     every artifact BUT the newest, one byte flipped in each:  rejected|1|missing

     4 of 4 were REJECTED, 0 matched the source digest, and
     0 produced a database that was not the source.
     what a refusal actually said, from the restore's own log:
      Error: no matching backup files available
      Error: no matching backup files available
     damage to REDUNDANT artifacts is NOT survivable here: with the newest artifact intact and only older ones damaged, the restore still refused (nonzero exit, no output file). A replica is therefore all-or-nothing in practice -- a single unreadable artifact can take a restore with it, which is a reason to keep more than one copy rather than to trust a partial one.
     The three newest-artifact cases measure whether damage is DETECTED; the
     fourth measures what a surviving restore is worth. The distinction
     matters because the first three have no substitute available, so they
     cannot tell 'checksum verified' apart from 'gave up'.
     What no case produced is the outcome that would be undetectable: a
     restore that exited 0 with a database that is not the source.

     THE OPERATIONAL CONSEQUENCE, which is why this is a gate and not a note:
     a restore's success cannot be read off litestream's exit status, in either
     direction. It has to be read off the CONTENT of the restored file, which is
     what (rows, sum, distinct) plus PRAGMA integrity_check is for -- and this
     gate runs both, on the source and on every restored file, instead of
     naming them in prose.

